{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get to the episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a heads up we have two new items at the \nmerch store. There’s a link in the description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes are inevitable predictions of \ngeneral relativity—our best theory of space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time and gravity. But they clash in \nmultiple ways with quantum mechanics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16680,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our equally successful description of the \nsubatomic world. One such clash is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21520,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole information paradox—and a proposed \nsolution—black hole complementarity—may forced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28280,
    "dDurationMs": 5641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us to radically rethink what it even \nmeans for something to exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39800,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that our universe is fundamentally \nself-consistent—otherwise what are we even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44080,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing trying to science it. But that means when a contradiction appears in our scientific  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "description of the universe, we know something is \nwrong with that description. It’s really exciting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when this happens because the nature of the \ninconsistency can point the way to a better, more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61280,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encompassing scientific description. Black holes \nare one of the favourite tools of the theoretical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67400,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicist because they lead to multiple \ninconsistencies between general relativity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and quantum mechanics, and so may be our best \npath to the grander theory that unites the two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77680,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The conflict we looked at recently is the black \nhole information paradox, and it’s not a bad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82440,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea to watch that episode before this. In it, \nour intrepid heroes of the gedankenexperiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice and Bob, discovered that black holes must \nviolate either a fundamental principle of general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity or of quantum mechanics. When Alice \ncarries a quantum bit—a qubit—into a black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "she witnesses the qubit cross the event \nhorizon. She must, because according to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106800,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the equivalence principle—a founding axiom of \ngeneral relativity—Alice can’t sense anything  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111800,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unusual when crossing that horizon. Meanwhile, \nBob, watching from afar, has to either witness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the qubit escape in the Hawking radiation leaked \nas the black hole evaporates, or the qubit vanish forever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that evaporation. In the first case, \nthe qubit is duplicated—it’s both inside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and outside the black hole. In the second it’s \nannihilated. Either way, a foundational principle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135240,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics—conservation of quantum \ninformation or unitarity appears to be violated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because both the equivalence principle \nand unitarity are fundamental to their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146040,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "respective theories, we know something must be \nwrong with our understanding of what happens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to quantum information in a black hole. \nThere have been various proposed solutions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154280,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but today I’m going to focus on one of the \nearliest, and perhaps the least intuitive—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s black hole complementarity, formulated \nby Leonard Susskind and others in the early 90s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163720,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black hole complementarity states that there \nactually is no contradiction. It proposes that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169040,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s fine for the same quantum information to be \ninside the black hole as measured by one observer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174360,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and frozen on the surface or radiated \naway in Hawking radiation according to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178080,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another. And according to “BHC” this is fine \nbecause no one can ever observe both states,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so no one can prove that unitarity \nwas broken, which means it … wasn’t?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get our heads around this, let’s start by \nmaking the conflict much more precise. To do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195560,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we’re going to use the same black hole map \nthat proper black hole theorists like to use—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199760,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose diagram. Without a black hole, a Penrose \ndiagram looks like this. Up is roughly speaking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the forward time direction, and left and right \nare roughly one spatial dimension. But space and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time are rescaled so they bend into each other \nand pile up towards the boundaries. Tick marks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214520,
    "dDurationMs": 6722,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are drawn closer together so that the border of the graph represents infinite distance and infinite past or future. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221242,
    "dDurationMs": 4518,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of this is done in just such a way so that \nlight will always travel a 45 degree path. All  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225760,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sub-lightspeed travel has to take a steeper \nslope—more time taken to travel less space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232440,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Near an event horizon of a black hole \nwe can think of spacetime as being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235280,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinitely stretched from the point of view of \na distant observer. That means we can just say  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239880,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of these boundaries is our event horizon, \nand add the interior of the black hole on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other side. In these coordinates, the central \nsingularity looks like the top of the Penrose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diagram is cut off—that represents the cessation \nof space and time inside the black hole. For now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254600,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll have to take my word that this is a valid \nway to draw the spacetime of a universe with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259120,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, but we have other videos on the \nPenrose diagram if you need more convincing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what Alice’s black hole expedition \nlooks like on the Penrose diagram. Both She  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270040,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Bob move up in time, while Alice and \nthe qubit also move closer to the event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. Light from the qubit reaches Alice \nand then Bob, carrying information about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279920,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qubit’s location—its past location \nby the time Alice or Bob see it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Approaching the event horizon, those photons \nstill reach Alice quickly but take longer and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "longer to reach Bob. Photons traveling from \njust above the event horizon only reach Bob  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293880,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the far future. No photon emitted below the \nevent horizon can ever reach Bob, so to him  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299840,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the qubit and Alice are frozen just above \nthe event horizon. Those photons emitted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside the black hole are doomed to hit the \nsingularity, as is the qubit. As is Alice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311960,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The diagram we’ve been using is for a black \nhole that’s always been there and always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315560,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be. Real black holes typically form \nfrom collapsed stars, and they also leak  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation until they disappear. Here’s \nhow we might depict such a black hole. We have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Penrose diagram for the universe where the \nblack hole forms somewhere in space when a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329960,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapsing star forms an event horizon. Then \nit evaporates by Hawking radiation. We only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335040,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need half of this map because, well, nothing \ngoing in one side ever comes out the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at just the qubit’s path. According \nto both Alice and Bob it falls and reaches the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346840,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon. According to just Alice it enters \nthe black hole and hits the singularity. Bob,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the other hand, sees it freeze on the \nhorizon and emerge again as Hawking radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It emerges only after the black hole is at least \nhalf-evaporated because, according to physicist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362920,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don Page, before that point the information in \nthe emitted radiation is hopelessly scrambled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368880,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From a perspective outside space and time, the \nquantum bit in some sense exists at all of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375960,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime points, but does it ever exist in \ntwo physical locations simultaneously—at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same instant time? Well there’s no absolute \ndefinition of “simultaneous” in Einstein’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative universe. But these lines on the Penrose \ndiagram could be considered to describe different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spatial locations at the same moment in \ntime. Therefore, for anything duplicated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on one of these lines, the copies can be \nthought of as existing at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, before it hits the event horizon \nthere’s only one qubit. After the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole evaporates there’s only one qubit—the \none leaked out in Hawking photons. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412440,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between its entry into the black hole and \nthe black hole’s evaporation we can argue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the qubit exists simultaneously \nin two places, violating unitarity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to this is to really dig into what \nwe mean by “existing simultaneously”. Due  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the finite travel time of light, we can \nonly confirm simultaneous existence at two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime points after the light from both \nreaches us. On our original Penrose diagram,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441120,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we only have information about the parts of the \nuniverse from which signals traveling at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light or lower could reach us—that’s our \npast light-cone. This is the only region in which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452520,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can verify simultaneity—and we can only verify \nthat things existed simultaneously after the fact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460280,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we try to do that for our duplicated \nqubits, we see that there is no past light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464640,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cone—no possible observer—who can ever verify \nthat both exist at the same time. Alice sees one,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob sees the other, but no one can ever see \nboth. Black hole complementarity argues that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the impossibility of any one observer \nmeasuring both qubits means that there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482520,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no violation of unitarity, so there’s no \ncontradiction. Before we pick that apart,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486880,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s make sure it’s really impossible for any \none observer to see both the Hawking-radiated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the swallowed versions of the qubit.\nPhysicists Bill Hayden and John Preskill  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figured out the best chance of one observer seeing \nboth. The thought experiment goes like this:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice jumps into the black hole with the quantum \nbit just before the black hole is half evaporated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because she knows that only after this so-called \nPage time can quantum information get back out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Below the event horizon she tries to send the \nqubit upwards. She knows it can’t re-cross the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon, but it will slow the qubit’s \ndescent to give Bob more time to catch it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now Bob also drops into the black hole. He \ntimes the leap exquisitely so that he catches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Hawking-radiated qubit on its way out, and hopes to also see the swallowed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537880,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qubit once inside. And … he misses it. Even \nwith the most perfectly timed experiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hayden and Preskill show that Bob will always \nbarely miss being able to see both qubits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550560,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it seems that nature is working awfully \nhard to make it impossible for anyone to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554040,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see both versions of the qubit. So maybe \nthe unobservability of the cloned qubits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is telling us something fundamental. That would \nbe the argument of black hole complementarity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564320,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which states that, because it’s \nimpossible for anyone to observe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both qubits, there’s no contradiction—no \nviolation of unitarity—for both to exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This sounds like some sort of weird \nquantum stuff. And complementarity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is indeed fundamental to quantum mechanics. \nFor example, there are complementary quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583920,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties like position and momentum that \ncan never be measured perfectly at the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time. Or complementary descriptions like the \nwave-like versus particle-like behavior of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quantum object. The word complementarity \nimplies a connection to quantum mechanics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the connection isn’t clear. For black \nhole complementarity there are different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603240,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretations, which are still argued over, \nand which aren’t even necessarily mutually exclusive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610640,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So interpretation 1): If black \nhole complementarity is right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614400,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it may be telling us that, while unitarity and the \nconservation of quantum information always hold,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620360,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way they hold is relative to a given \nobserver. Alice will always find that quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625680,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics works perfectly and that there \nare never any contradictions. So will Bob,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but for him quantum mechanics might work \nperfectly in a different way. The key is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637440,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that if Alice and Bob can never communicate, \nno contradiction is ever discovered. If this is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right then it’s telling us something about what \nthe wavefunction and quantum information really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647960,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "represent and that our description of the world \ndepends quite radically on our reference frame.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655040,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even a concept as basic as the existence of a \nquantum state may be relative to the observer.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not the only hint at this uncomfortable \nidea—for example, the existence of a particle can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666800,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depend on the acceleration of an observer, as we \nsaw in our Unruh radiation episode. Interestingly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672840,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both Unruh radiation and black hole \ncomplementarity involve uncrossable horizons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So interpretation 2) for black hole complementarity \nis that the interior and exterior descriptions of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum information are, in a sense, \nequivalent. Or rather they are different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "descriptions of what is fundamentally the one \nquantum system. There’s no duplication because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the interior and exterior of the black hole \nare different descriptions of the same abstract  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum system. This is a form of holography, \nin which a lower dimensional system can be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equally-well described as a system one dimension \nhigher—a boundary and its interior become  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different ways to talk about the same thing. We’ve \ntalked about holographic principle before—it’s an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718760,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea that can be extended well beyond black \nholes, even suggesting that the interior of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722920,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe may have a dual and complementary \ndescription on its infinitely distant surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729200,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black hole complementarity is by no means \nthe accepted solution to the black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733800,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information paradox. We haven’t talked \nabout black hole firewalls yet—in which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an extreme energy screen just above the event \nhorizon fries anything that tries to enter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743840,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eliminating any duplication of qubits but also \nviolating the equivalence principle. In an episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming very soon we’ll see why some physicists \nthink the firewall must exist, and also why the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "firewall may not free us from the strangeness \nof black hole complementarity or vice versa."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, yeah, black holes are contradictions. \nThey are holes in the universe and in our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767000,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding of it. But through those holes \nwe’re glimpsing grander visions of what our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770760,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe might really be. For example, that \nthere’s a sort of extreme relativity to our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "description of the world, or that the interiors \nof black holes and of universes may be in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weird way equivalent to their surfaces—each the \nwarped reflection of a complementary spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789360,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are lots of ways to support this channel. \nLiking and subscribing helps a ton. Watching when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you get the notification is super valuable for \nappeasing the almighty YouTube algorithm. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps the thing that's been the most important \nfor the longevity of the series is Patreon. Even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803320,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a couple of bucks a month is a big deal for us \nand it gets you access to the spacetime Discord  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and our monthly live hangout. And if you're \nalready a supporter, we can't thank you enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, today, I want to give an extra special \nthank you to John Sronce who supporting us at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818040,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Big Bang level. John, we've used some \nof your contribution to beat this episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along with this shout out to be included on the \nevent horizon of the Cygnus X-1 black hole. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in around ten to the power of 71 years, this \nwill be radiated back out as hawking radiation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ensuring that even the end of the \nuniverse is reminded of your generosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do you do fellow simulations? Before we end \nthe episode, I just wanted to let you know that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are adding a couple new permanent items \nto the merch store. Back by popular demand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the return of the Be Quiet The Devs Will \nNotice T-shirt, which now also available for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first time as a hoodie. And we also have \nour new How Do You Do fellow simulations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "T-shirt and sweatshirt. For those of you who'd \nlike to blend more seamlessly into the simulation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869320,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever your stance is on your virtual existence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872000,
    "dDurationMs": 8446,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we've got you covered at our very virtual \nmarketplace over at pbsspacetime.com/shop."
    } ]
  } ]
}
